 2025 about **$57.4B**, and fiscal 2026 about **$67.4B**. That is roughly **+6%**, then **+8%**, then **+17%** year over year. Cloud is doing most of the heavy lifting: Oracle cloud revenue reached about **$34.0B in FY2026, up 39%**, and OCI infrastructure revenue was up **77%** for the full year. citeturn410021search4turn410021search5\n\nNetSuite specifically is also still growing at a very healthy rate. Oracle reports it as part of SaaS rather than as a standalone company, so we do not get a full NetSuite P&L anymore. But the cleanest comparable number is quarterly NetSuite Cloud ERP revenue: about **$700M in Q4 FY2023, +22% YoY**; about **$800M in Q4 FY2024, +19%**; and about **$1.0B in Q4 FY2025, +18%**. So the important thing is not just that NetSuite got bigger — it has been sustaining **high-teens/low-20s growth at a much larger base**. citeturn410021search6turn410021search7turn410021search2\n\ngenui{\"chart\":{\"content\":{\"chartType\":\"line\",\"meta\":{\"title\":\"NetSuite Cloud ERP quarterly revenue\",\"description\":\"Oracle-reported Q4 NetSuite Cloud ERP SaaS revenue, fiscal 2023–2025.\"},\"xKey\":\"year\",\"series\":[{\"dataKey\":\"revenue\",\"label\":\"NetSuite Q4 revenue\",\"axisLabel\":\"Revenue\",\"valuePrefix\":\"$\",\"valueSuffix\":\"B\",\"valueFormat\":\"raw\"}],\"data\":[{\"year\":\"FY2023 Q4\",\"revenue\":0.7},{\"year\":\"FY2024 Q4\",\"revenue\":0.8},{\"year\":\"FY2025 Q4\",\"revenue\":1.0}]}}}\n\n**MHI is harder because it is private.** I would not pretend we have audited revenue numbers for Myers-Holum; we don't. Their public materials historically talked about roughly **60% annual growth** during the earlier NetSuite expansion period, and they went from about 25 employees in 2018 to more than 260 by 2021. citeturn716143search5turn716143search17 More importantly for the period you care about, their market position has remained extremely strong: they won **NetSuite Alliance Partner of the Year in 2023, 2024, and 2025**, with the 2025 win being their fifth consecutive Alliance Partner of the Year recognition. citeturn716143search19turn474697search1\n\nThere is one wrinkle I would pay attention to. Third-party workforce intelligence estimates suggest MHI headcount peaked around 2023 and then contracted through 2025/26. That source estimates roughly **412 employees in 2023, 376 in 2024, and around 290–294 by 2025/26**. MHI's own public pages still describe a team of 350–425+ depending on the page, so I would treat the exact headcount as fuzzy, not gospel. citeturn716143search2turn716143search3turn716143search12\n\nMy read on the three-year pattern is therefore:\n\n**Oracle:** accelerating hard because cloud infrastructure and AI demand have become enormous growth engines.\n\n**NetSuite:** very steady, durable high-teens growth; it looks like a mature ERP platform that is still expanding significantly rather than flattening out.\n\n**MHI:** not necessarily \"grow headcount at 60% forever\" anymore. It looks more like they went through a hypergrowth build-out, then moved into a **more mature, selective, higher-leverage phase** while continuing to win major NetSuite partner awards and complex implementations. That is actually potentially favorable for your interview: a Principal Consultant role in that environment is less \"we need bodies because we're exploding\" and more \"we need senior people who can protect delivery quality, commercial health, and client outcomes as the practice scales.\"\n\nAnd there is a very good interview implication hiding in here: **you are interviewing into an ecosystem that is not shrinking.** NetSuite itself is still growing around high teens, Oracle is accelerating, and MHI remains one of the strongest Alliance partners in that ecosystem. That makes their emphasis on experienced Principal-level delivery leadership make a lot of sense.\n\nI can also dig one level deeper and build you a **2023 → 2026 MHI/NetSuite/Oracle growth timeline** showing awards, headcount signals, revenue growth, and what changed strategically each year."]}, "create_time": 1789363783.153478, "end_turn": true, "id": "7773d521-175d-4897-95a4-293b826e135c", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": "([SEC](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1341439/000119312526277521/orcl-20260531.htm?utm_source=chatgpt.com))", "end_idx": 616, "error": null, "fallback_items": null, "items": [{"attribution": "SEC", "attribution_segments": null, "attributions": null, "hue": null, "pub_date": 1782086400, "refs": [{"ref_index": 4, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 0}, {"ref_index": 5, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 0}], "snippet": "", "supporting_websites": [{"attribution": "Oracle Investor Relations", "pub_date": null, "snippet": "", "title": "Oracle - Oracle Announces Record Q4 and FY 2026 Results Driven by Cloud Infrastructure & Cloud Applications", "url": "https://investor.oracle.com/investor-news/news-details/2026/Oracle-Announces-Record-Q4-and-FY-2026-Results-Driven-by-Cloud-Infrastructure--Cloud-Applications/?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}], "title": "10-K", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1341439/000119312526277521/orcl-20260531.htm?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}], "matched_text": "citeturn410021search4turn410021search5", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": ["https://investor.oracle.com/investor-news/news-details/2026/Oracle-Announces-Record-Q4-and-FY-2026-Results-Driven-by-Cloud-Infrastructure--Cloud-Applications/", "https://investor.oracle.com/investor-news/news-details/2026/Oracle-Announces-Record-Q4-and-FY-2026-Results-Driven-by-Cloud-Infrastructure--Cloud-Applications/?utm_source=chatgpt.com", "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1341439/000119312526277521/orcl-20260531.htm", "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1341439/000119312526277521/orcl-20260531.htm?utm_source=chatgpt.com"], "start_idx": 574, "status": "done", "style": null, "type": "grouped_webpages"}, {