789363805.203549, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "thoughts", "source_analysis_msg_id": "b32a9414-b640-4043-be0f-7b697b92aa91", "thoughts": [{"chunks": [], "content": "", "finished": true, "summary": "Searched 6 websites"}]}, "create_time": 1789363787.228533, "end_turn": false, "id": "229abacf-2ac2-4eb5-8b1f-2d1edc107247", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "classifier_response": "default", "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "hide_inline_actions": true, "inline_cot_expandable_content": {"search_result_groups": [{"domain": "www.ft.com", "entries": [{"attribution": "www.ft.com", "pub_date": 1789071890.393, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 69, "ref_type": "news", "turn_index": 410021}, "snippet": "Oracle reported a significant surge in its data centre revenue, with its infrastructure unit earning $7.4 billion last quarter. Year-on-year growth in the broader cloud business is projected between 64% and 70%, surpassing analyst expectations. This reflects Oracle’s transformation from a software provider to a key AI infrastructure supplier, notably with a massive $300 billion contract with OpenAI. The company brought online 850 megawatts of data centre capacity and completed six of eight buildings at its new Texas campus. Total quarterly revenue increased by 30% to $19.3 billion, with $30 billion in new contracts and a $664 billion contract pipeline. Despite this growth, Oracle faces financial strain, investing $28.5 billion in capital expenditures—up from $8.5 billion the previous year, and reporting negative free cash flow of $5 billion. S&P downgraded Oracle's credit rating due to reliance on a few clients and uncertain AI business profitability. The company also faces delays due to regulatory hurdles and permitting issues. To counter financial concerns, Oracle has utilized customer prepayments for chips and conducted substantial layoffs. Its stock rose 6% after the earnings report but remains down nearly 50% from last year’s peak.", "title": "Oracle's data centre revenue surges as AI strategy accelerates", "type": "search_result", "url": "https://www.ft.com/content/9df99d0b-6ef6-4530-8771-8b220f740e74"}], "type": "search_result_group"}, {"domain": "www.reuters.com", "entries": [{"attribution": "www.reuters.com", "pub_date": 1789071242, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 70, "ref_type": "news", "turn_index": 410021}, "snippet": "Oracle exceeded Wall Street expectations in its latest quarterly results, reporting a significant increase in its revenue backlog and a smaller-than-anticipated cash burn, providing optimism regarding its AI investments. The company secured over $30 billion in new AI cloud contracts in the first fiscal quarter, boosting its total revenue backlog to $664 billion, surpassing analysts' estimates. A key factor behind this growth is Oracle's expanding data center infrastructure, which is attracting more enterprise cloud contracts driven by an increasing demand for AI services. Importantly, the majority of these new contracts do not require substantial new capital investments, allowing Oracle to maintain its annual spending targets.\n\nDespite earlier investor concerns about Oracle’s heavy capital spending and its Stargate project delays, the company reported a negative free cash flow of $5.40 billion, significantly better than the expected $9.56 billion. First-quarter capital expenditure reached $28.50 billion, with $11.36 billion offset by customer prepayments. Oracle posted a 30% year-over-year revenue increase to $19.3 billion and adjusted earnings of $1.92 per share, both beating expectations. The company also raised its fiscal 2027 earnings forecast to $8.10 per share. These positive developments helped boost Oracle's stock 4% in extended trading.", "thumbnail_url": "https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/Bz2X_1Z9xu5tqcLfAKeQ78jq0w8wk95F7gTB7gbx9hMyxDfs04M5cLiTh