earch_result", "url": "https://myersholum.com/news/myers-holum-awarded-oracle-netsuite-alliance-partner-of-the-year-at-suiteworld-2023"}, {"attribution": "myersholum.com", "pub_date": 1719360000, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 9, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 184140}, "snippet": "", "title": "Toys For Trucks, Inc. + Myers-Holum, Inc.: Winter 2024 Alliance Partner Spotlight Award Winner", "type": "search_result", "url": "https://myersholum.com/blogs/toys-for-trucks-myers-holum-winter-2024-alliance-partner"}, {"attribution": "myersholum.com", "pub_date": null, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 10, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 184140}, "snippet": "", "title": "Myers-Holum and ProScope Partner to Advance Next-Generation Project Management - Myers-Holum", "type": "search_result", "url": "https://myersholum.com/news/myers-holum-and-proscope-partner-to-advance-next-generation-project-management"}, {"attribution": "myersholum.com", "pub_date": 1713744000, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 11, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 184140}, "snippet": "", "title": "Monolith + Myers-Holum, Inc.: Winter 2024 Alliance Partner Spotlight Award Winner", "type": "search_result", "url": "https://myersholum.com/blogs/monolith-myers-holum-winter-2024-alliance-partner"}, {"attribution": "myersholum.com", "pub_date": 1783468800, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 12, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 184140}, "snippet": "", "title": "Your ERP and Your Budget Shouldn’t Be Strangers", "type": "search_result", "url": "https://myersholum.com/blogs/your-erp-and-your-budget-shouldnt-be-strangers"}, {"attribution": "myersholum.com", "pub_date": 1782172800, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 13, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 184140}, "snippet": "", "title": "Why “Right-Size” Beats Biggest in EPM Consulting", "type": "search_result", "url": "https://myersholum.com/blogs/why-right-size-beats-biggest-in-epm-consulting"}], "type": "search_result_group"}, {"domain": "nlcorp.app.netsuite.com", "entries": [{"attribution": "nlcorp.app.netsuite.com", "pub_date": 1710931453, "ref_id": {"ref_index": 64, "ref_type": "search", "turn_index": 184140}, "snippet": "", "title": "NetSuite 2024.1 Release Notes", "type": "search_result", "url": "https://nlcorp.app.netsuite.com/core/media/media.nl?_xt=.pdf&c=NLCORP&h=Lkm2ESy3gDlTtOsIDOd_t05ISeaEmFR2AGJu2O8EC9t2aagv&id=423519000"}], "type": "search_result_group"}], "thinking_effort": "standard", "tool_icons": ["https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=nlcorp.app.netsuite.com&sz=128"], "turn_exchange_id": "233133b1-3615-4b48-b0ce-68c7b0050458", "turn_id": "4489d4eb-a7b2-4a6f-bf67-4f6baf57a47b", "working_turn_id": "233133b1-3615-4b48-b0ce-68c7b0050458"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {"real_author": "tool:web"}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yeah. And the **AI question is actually the more important second half**. Looking at 2023 through now, the story is: the ecosystem has moved from “cloud ERP growth” into **cloud ERP + analytics + embedded AI + extensibility**.\n\nHere’s the concise timeline.\n\n**2023 — expansion and analytics.** Oracle finished FY2023 at about **$50B revenue**, up 18%. NetSuite Q4 Cloud ERP revenue was about **$700M, up 22% YoY**. MHI won NetSuite Alliance Partner of the Year again and was already pushing heavily into NetSuite Analytics Warehouse and complex vertical implementations. That tells you the market was still primarily about cloud ERP adoption, data consolidation, analytics, integrations, and scaling companies off fragmented systems. citeturn511428search7turn184140search8\n\n**2024 — growth continued, and generative AI entered the actual product.** Oracle reached **$53B**, up 6%; NetSuite Q4 ERP revenue reached roughly **$800M, up 19%**. NetSuite 2024.1 introduced **Text Enhance**, one of the first obvious generative-AI capabilities directly inside the ERP workflow. MHI remained Alliance Partner of the Year and kept winning vertical awards, including automotive and analytics-related implementations. citeturn511428search8turn184140search64turn184140search0turn184140search9\n\n**2025 — NetSuite AI became more than a writing assistant.** Oracle finished FY2025 at **$57.4B, up 8%**, while NetSuite Q4 Cloud ERP reached about **$1B, up 18%**. Oracle’s total cloud revenue was accelerating much faster than the company overall. At the NetSuite level, AI expanded into operational use cases rather than just generated text. Today’s NetSuite AI portfolio includes things such as Customer 360, Intelligent Close Manager, Item Creation Assistant, Narrative Insights, transaction matching, SuiteAnswers assistance, Prompt Studio, and developer tooling. citeturn511428search1turn511428search2\n\nAnd critically for **you**, Oracle opened the platform itself to AI development. SuiteScript 2.1 now has the **`N/llm` module**, letting developers call Oracle-hosted LLMs from SuiteScript. That's a fundamental architectural change. AI is not merely a bolt-on UI feature anymore; NetSuite customers and partners can build AI behavior into ERP workflows programmatically. citeturn511428search11turn511428search19\n\n**2026 — Oracle went into AI-infrastructure overdrive.** FY2026 revenue jumped to **$67.4B, +17%**. Cloud revenue hit **$34B, +39%**; OCI infrastructure revenue grew **77%**. Oracle's remaining performance obligations exploded to **$638B**, with management explicitly attributing much of the increase to large AI contracts. So Oracle is no longer dabbling in AI. They're pouring enormous capital into the compute layer underneath it. citeturn511428search9\n\nNetSuite is consequently becoming much more agent-like. Current 2026 documentation shows things like **Ask Oracle**, which can decompose requests into multiple jobs, operate across documents, and combine the results. NetSuite's current AI documentation now explicitly separates **generative AI, SuiteScript AI modules, skills and agents, AI integrations, and traditional machine learning**. citeturn511428search6turn511428search13\n\n### Where MHI sits\n\nMHI's public positioning is important. They aren't simply a vanilla NetSuite implementation shop. They describe themselves as NetSuite's largest Alliance Partner and emphasize **customization, systems integration, analytics/data warehousing, ecommerce, payments and proprietary IP**. They've also built a broad partner ecosystem around NetSuite rather than insisting everything must be native. citeturn511428search12turn184140search7\n\nThat's exactly the kind of partner that can adapt to AI, because AI increases the importance of **data quality, integrations, business-process understanding and orchestration** rather than eliminating those things.\n\nAnd there's something I think directly connects to the role you're interviewing for. MHI is currently emphasizing a delivery model where **senior practitioners remain involved from alignment through delivery**, rather than architects disappearing after the sale and dumping the project onto a junior implementation team. citeturn184140search13\n\nThat's basically the conversation we just had tonight.\n\n### The bigger AI answer\n\nI would **not** look at NetSuite and think, “AI is going to replace this ERP.”\n\nI would think:\n\n**ERP becomes the transactional truth layer underneath AI.**\n\nAI still needs to know:\n\n**Who is the customer?  \nWhat inventory exists?  \nWhat did we promise?  \nWhat did we ship?  \nWhat does it cost?  \nWho approved it?  \nWhat is the PO?  \nWhat's the BOM?  \nWhat is the GL impact?**\n\nThat's NetSuite.\n\nThe change is that people increasingly won't need to navigate every screen manually to interact with that truth. Agents, prompts, APIs, SuiteScript/LLM functions, automation and external AI systems will increasingly **read from, reason over and transact against the ERP**.\n\nAnd that makes the Principal Consultant job more interesting rather than less relevant. Somebody still has to establish:\n\n**requirements → scope → process → system design → data → permissions → controls → integrations → validation → governance.**\n\nOtherwise the AI simply automates a badly designed process faster.\n\nSo if I'm looking at this from the standpoint of **your next five years**, I actually like where you're positioned. You're not coming in as “20-year NetSuite guy who now has to learn AI.” Your experience is moving in the opposite direction:\n\n**ERP implementation leadership + integration architecture + manufacturing domain knowledge + actual AI/automation building experience.**\n\nThat's a pretty unusual intersection.\n\nThe risk I would watch isn't whether NetSuite survives AI. I think that's the wrong risk. The real risk is whether a consulting firm stays stuck selling **labor-intensive configuration hours** while Oracle makes more configuration, analysis and development AI-assisted.\n\nFrom what I can see publicly, **MHI looks substantially better positioned than a generic NetSuite body shop** because of its integration, analytics, proprietary-IP, complex implementation and solution-architecture capabilities. I haven't yet found enough public evidence to say that MHI itself has a mature, differentiated **AI consulting practice**. That's the question I'd still investigate. citeturn184140search7turn511428search12\n\nAnd that could eventually be a very interesting Andrew question — not Wednesday's opening question, but once the conversation is going well:\n\n**“As NetSuite moves further into embedded AI and agentic capabilities, how is MHI thinking about that within the practice?”**\n\nThat isn't you trying to sell him an AI framework. It's a Principal Consultant asking where the practice is going.\n\nThat question could tell you a *lot*."]}, "create_time": 1789363853.284292, "end_turn": true, "id": "032a4529-ea83-43de-8a03-5ccded143227", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [],