ed": true, "dictation_original_text": "Yeah, that's why I wanted to break it down like that. And again, with it sitting on top, that layer. Again, I knew, and of course it's Oracle NetSuite, bro. You're the fucking OG of this shit, right? It's gonna grow, right? But it's growing in a way where I'm taking advantage of it in the right way, right? I'm understanding, I'm learning it. It's gonna sit on top. It's automation. It's principal consultant of the AI and the NetSuite ERP. Two separate layers.", "message_source": null, "request_id": "183bf221-83fa-491b-bcb0-80ac879c7eed", "triggered_by_system_hint_suggestion": false, "turn_exchange_id": "91a08bb3-4d1f-4eb5-bae5-c589fa204144", "turn_id": "c3bf991b-b4df-418f-a98d-c7264eddecff", "working_turn_id": "91a08bb3-4d1f-4eb5-bae5-c589fa204144"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789364796.787076, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Exactly. Two layers, and your advantage is being able to bridge them.\n\n**Layer 1: ERP delivery.** NetSuite/Oracle remains the system of record: requirements, scope, process, controls, data, integrations, implementation lifecycle, governance.\n\n**Layer 2: AI/automation.** That sits on top of the ERP and augments how work gets done: reasoning, workflow assistance, analytics, agents, decision support, document handling, automation.\n\nYou do not have to collapse those into one identity. That is actually cleaner.\n\nYou can be a **Principal Consultant in the ERP layer** and, over time, become the person who understands how the **AI layer should be designed and governed around it**.\n\nThat is much more durable than chasing whatever standalone AI tool is hot at the moment, because the ERP layer persists. The AI layer evolves on top of it.\n\nAnd that is probably the cleanest way to think about your next few years: **master the Principal seat inside MHI, then let the AI/applied architecture layer become your differentiator and eventually your leadership lane.** memcite"]}, "create_time": 1789364790.839486, "end_turn": true, "id": "4c63e883-1ac2-484e-bdbd-dde1bdefc4a5", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": null, "end_idx": 1072, "invalid": false, "matched_text": "memcite", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 1063, "type": "hidden"}], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "02387d5c-afa3-40f5-94b5-85200e2a83b2", "request_id": "183bf221-83fa-491b-bcb0-80ac879c7eed", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "91a08bb3-4d1f-4eb5-bae5-c589fa204144", "turn_id": "83a59727-f9ff-4561-9dcd-e28ae58c779b", "working_turn_id": "91a08bb3-4d1f-4eb5-bae5-c589fa204144"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789364796.483833, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for a second", "content_type": "reasoning_recap"}, "create_time": 1789364791.547588, "end_turn": false, "id": "02387d5c-afa3-40f5-94b5-85200e2a83b2", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finished_duration_sec": 0, "hide_inline_actions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "f1b63051-5e71-4434-90a4-9c6c8f174863", "reasoning_end_time": 1789364791.463558, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1789364790.8683465, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "183bf221-83fa-491b-bcb0-80ac879c7eed", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "91a08bb3-4d1f-4eb5-bae5-c589fa204144", "turn_id": "83a59727-f9ff-4561-9dcd-e28ae58c779b", "working_turn_id": "91a08bb3-4d1f-4eb5-bae5-c589fa204144"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Thank you, because that's what, that's what I could transition to, is basically the reason why, the what, the how. What I was doing with Andrew and Conviance was learning AI and basically understanding how to do ERP automation. So the big reason why I'm back into NetSuite is not because I didn't want to do it. It's because of how I work as a principal consultant, is the golden, the golden goose of having both your eggs, or both your fucking, have your cake and eat it too, is I'm layered in the biggest fucking software as a system service, SaaS service, with Oracle and NetSuite giving me more tools that I already understand at the hardest level, from Bedrock Azure, multiple agents, multiple everything, building my own from scratch that now sit on top of NetSuite. That's not only fucking valuable, that's just fucking cool."]}, "create_time": 1789364856.9844599, "end_turn": null, "id": "bc4decab-3bf6-4ddd-ba71-d122ffbe11be", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "dictation": true, "dictation_auto_submitted": true, "dictation_original_text": "Thank you, because that's what, that's what I could transition to, is basically the reason why, the what, the how. What I was doing with Andrew and Conviance was learning AI and basically understanding how to do ERP automation. So the big reason why I'm back into NetSuite is not because I didn't want to do it. It's because of how I work as a principal consultant, is the golden, the golden goose of having both your eggs, or both your fucking, have your cake and eat it too, is I'm layered in the biggest fucking software as a system service, SaaS service, with Oracle and NetSuite giving me more tools that I already understand at the hardest level, from Bedrock Azure, multiple agents, multiple everything, building my own from scratch that now sit on top of NetSuite. That's not only fucking valuable, that's just fucking cool.", "message_source": null, "request_id": "4fad887f-c56c-4a47-b5be-13f48e1206ae", "triggered_by_system_hint_suggestion": false, "turn_exchange_id": "8222276f-32f1-4747-9e55-6bd72763d63a", "turn_id": "720ac9c2-ffa6-4e8d-9e91-f4643be300b1", "working_turn_id": "8222276f-32f1-4747-9e55-6bd72763d63a"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789364874.601323, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yeah — that’s the right synthesis.\n\nYou weren’t leaving ERP to “go do AI.” You were building the second layer.\n\nNow you’re coming back into NetSuite with something materially different than before: deep ERP delivery judgment **plus** applied AI architecture experience across external platforms, agents, automation, orchestration, and analytics. And Oracle/NetSuite is increasingly exposing native places for that capability to plug in.\n\nSo the model is clean:\n\n**NetSuite/Oracle = transactional backbone and control layer**  \n**AI/automation = intelligence and leverage layer**  \n**You = the person who understands how to connect them responsibly**\n\nThat’s the “why” behind the move.\n\nAnd it is cool because you’re not learning AI in a vacuum. You’re attaching it to business processes you already understand at a very deep level: manufacturing, finance, procurement, order flow, integrations, permissions, data, implementation, governance.\n\nThat combination is where the value is.\n\nFor the interview, though, keep that as the **second-order upside**. First, Andrew needs to see: “this guy can run a Principal Consultant engagement.” Then, once that is obvious, the AI background becomes the thing that makes you unusually expandable inside the practice. memcite"]}, "create_time": 1789364858.0171, "end_turn": true, "id": "1c3b2396-713b-416e-8844-8137f42be1a3", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": null, "end_idx": 1265, "invalid": false, "matched_text": "memcite", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 1256, "type": "hidden"}], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "d16c42b1-51f8-4f80-b587-6fd6f3104d6d", "request_id": "4fad887f-c56c-4a47-b5be-13f48e1206ae", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "8222276f-32f1-4747-9e55-6bd72763d63a", "turn_id": "d3bf5d64-d645-4726-8141-fa84d12e7a8f", "working_turn_id": "8222276f-32f1-4747-9e55-6bd72763d63a"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789364874.322771, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for 11s", "content_type": "reasoning_recap"}, "create_time": 1789364869.085248, "end_turn": false, "id": "d16c42b1-51f8-4f80-b587-6fd6f3104d6d", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finished_duration_sec": 11, "hide_inline_actions": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "9e230488-1fe4-4b8f-9b50-c03d77c84cde", "reasoning_end_time": 1789364869.0802217, "reasoning_recap_type": "hide_all", "reasoning_start_time": 1789364858.0363727, "reasoning_status": "reasoning_ended", "request_id": "4fad887f-c56c-4a47-b5be-13f48e1206ae", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "8222276f-32f1-4747-9e55-6bd72763d63a", "turn_id": "d3bf5d64-d645-4726-8141-fa84d12e7a8f", "working_turn_id": "8222276f-32f1-4747-9e55-6bd72763d63a"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": null, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "user"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Most of all, it's what we talked about about me and Leon. I know the operations side, what the business wants. Leon's the developer, right? The fucking guy who makes the AI work. I know how to do both, technically. That's what makes me such a strong candidate overall, just a strong, knowledgeable person, is I know how to develop it. I can build it. I fucking build it from scratch. Do I want to do that? No. My value is in managing the whole fucking thing because I know both. And as always, I can relate to the client. MHI is one of those clients. Now, it's client— everybody says client first, right? You know, but being able to relate all this technical stack, operate it, outline it, get people to do it, document it, and see the outcomes is one of the most rewarding things in the fucking world. Because not many can switch their mind off from a technical person to an operation to shit, we got to sit in front of the client and tell them, this is what you got."]}, "create_time": 1789364933.415677, "end_turn": null, "id": "4bf898cf-890b-4a1e-82cf-8d538f7c54e2", "metadata": {"can_save": false, "dictation": true, "dictation_auto_submitted": true, "dictation_original_text": "Most of all, it's what we talked about about me and Leon. I know the operations side, what the business wants. Leon's the developer, right? The fucking guy who makes the AI work. I know how to do both, technically. That's what makes me such a strong candidate overall, just a strong, knowledgeable person, is I know how to develop it. I can build it. I fucking build it from scratch. Do I want to do that? No. My value is in managing the whole fucking thing because I know both. And as always, I can relate to the client. MHI is one of those clients. Now, it's client— everybody says client first, right? You know, but being able to relate all this technical stack, operate it, outline it, get people to do it, document it, and see the outcomes is one of the most rewarding things in the fucking world. Because not many can switch their mind off from a technical person to an operation to shit, we got to sit in front of the client and tell them, this is what you got.", "message_source": null, "request_id": "8d668c26-ca4e-4759-bbf5-e73b8deb9937", "triggered_by_system_hint_suggestion": false, "turn_exchange_id": "ab011964-c8d5-495c-a078-c0f098ba7e70", "turn_id": "e6984211-1bc4-4225-95a7-00625467e25d", "working_turn_id": "ab011964-c8d5-495c-a078-c0f098ba7e70"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789364945.624891, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["That’s the differentiator.\n\nYou can speak **three languages** fluently:\n\n**business/operations**, **technical architecture/build**, and **client delivery**.\n\nA lot of people are strong in one. Some can bridge two. The rarer profile is someone who can understand what the business is trying to achieve, translate that into a workable technical solution, know enough to challenge the developers and architects, and then sit in front of the client and explain the outcome in plain language.\n\nThat’s why your highest-value seat is not “the guy writing the code.” It’s the person owning the whole thing.\n\nYou can still go deep enough to know when the technical answer is bullshit, when the architecture is overcomplicated, when a requirement is being misunderstood, or when somebody is solving the wrong problem. But you don’t have to personally build every component to create value.\n\nFor MHI, that translates almost perfectly into the Principal Consultant posture we just defined:\n\n**understand the client → validate requirements and scope → shape the solution → organize the right specialists → govern execution → communicate clearly → deliver the business outcome.**\n\nAnd your AI background just widens the technical surface area you can govern.\n\nThat’s a strong executive-level story because it’s not “I can do everything.” It’s:\n\n**“I understand enough of every layer to lead the whole engagement intelligently.”** memcite"]}, "create_time": 1789364935.280087, "end_turn": true, "id": "bd9be456-699d-41d1-bfc3-cac17d43963b", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": null, "end_idx": 1425, "invalid": false, "matched_text": "memcite", "prefix": null, "prompt_text": null, "refs": [], "safe_urls": [], "start_idx": 1416, "type": "hidden"}], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "fb54e89c-63b3-4d3d-9735-5ae2f4d8168f", "request_id": "8d668c26-ca4e-4759-bbf5-e73b8deb9937", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "turn_exchange_id": "ab011964-c8d5-495c-a078-c0f098ba7e70", "turn_id": "d57e5484-7772-4d10-be29-610bd834a262", "working_turn_id": "ab011964-c8d5-495c-a078-c0f098ba7e70"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1789364945.212815, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": null, "content": {"content": "Worked for 